  "style": {
    "01_register": "UI labels, buttons, tabs and column heads are bare noun phrases (명사형) with no sentence ending, ideally <= 12 Hangul syllables (most 2-5). Short stat names are exactly the 2-syllable full names: 무력 통솔 지력 정치 매력. Use Koei Korean 삼국지 vocabulary throughout; Sino-Korean words are preferred over native or English loanwords in game terms (병량 not 식량, 금 not 골드, 무장 not 장교, 일기토 not 결투). Keep only established UI loanwords: 턴, 슬롯, 시나리오, 사운드, VS, 1x.",
    "02_system_messages": "Everything addressed to the player by the interface (ui.* hints, confirmations, errors; order.reason.*, army.reason.*, trade/donate/reward/prisoner.reason.*, error.*) uses formal polite 합쇼체: '도시를 선택하십시오.', '금이 부족합니다.', '계획 단계에서만 저장할 수 있습니다.' Never use 해요체 or 반말 here, and never address the player as 당신/너: omit the subject or say 아군/우리 (your cities -> 아군 도시, your officers -> 휘하 무장).",
    "03_narration": "Chronicle and battle logs (log.*, battle.log.*, battle.summary.*, prisoner.* results, duel.round.*, event.*.effect.*), scenario descriptions, bios and event narration use written 한다체 with a dignified historical tone: past tense for the chronicle ('{city}이(가) 함락되었다.'), vivid present for battle and duel play-by-play ('{unit}이(가) {target}을(를) 공격한다.'). Avoid modern slang and bureaucratic jargon; four-character idioms (사자성어) are welcome where natural.",
    "04_dialogue": "Quoted speech keeps a Joseon-drama register: retainers to their ruler use 하옵니다/하십시오체 ('주공, 도적들이 상단을 습격하고 있사옵니다.'); nobles to equals or envoys use 하오체 ('받아들이겠소?'); superiors to subordinates use 하게체; Black Mountain sworn brothers speak rough but loyal 해라체/하게체 among themselves and call their chief 형님; toward 형님 they may use rough 하오체 ('아우들이 영 들썩거리오', '일깨워 줍시다'), never 하옵니다체. The innkeeper and tavern rumors (rumor.*, officer.*.rumor) are hearsay in plain polite speech: '~라고들 합니다', '~라더군요'; commoners such as the innkeeper address the player as 나리, never 주공 (주공 is a retainer's word for his own lord). Address terms: player ruler = 주공; Liu Chengxuan = 전하 (third person 황자); Su Wanqing = 여왕 전하 (third person 여왕); Helian Bo = 대칸 (third person 칸). The vocative 주공 stands alone: never prefix the ruler's personal name ('{ruler} 주공,' is rude). When a {ruler} placeholder must appear in a retainer's speech, refer to the lord in the third person with honorifics ('{ruler} 주공께서 명하신 큰 주문') and keep the vocative bare ('주공, ...').",
    "05_event_choices": "Event choices (event.*.choice.N) are the ruler's decision as a short 한다체 declarative, Koei style: '병사를 보내 토벌한다', '무시한다', '돈을 주어 물러가게 한다 (금 300)'. Costs go at the end as '(금 300)' / '(병량 3000)'. event.choice.ok 'So be it.' = '그리하라.'",
    "06_particles": "Korean particles depend on whether the previous syllable has a final consonant, and placeholders ({officer}, {city}, {faction}, {target}, {commander}, {unit}, {winner}, {loser}, {sect}, {technique}, {name}, {ruler}, {talent}, {side}, {location}, {tactic}, {move}...) can end either way (철주/안문, 서랑기/청정). The engine has no particle selection, so: (1) prefer particle-invariant constructions: 의, 에, 에서, 에게, 께, 도, 만, 까지, 부터, 보다, 처럼, or restructure ('{city} 함락!' / '{faction}의 손에 {city} 함락'); (2) when a variable particle is unavoidable, write the paired form with the consonant variant first: 이(가), 을(를), 은(는), 과(와), (으)로, (이)라는, (이)다, 이여/여 -> '{officer}이(가) {city}을(를) 점령했다.' Never choose 가/를/는 from a single sample name. Do not put particles straight after numeric placeholders; attach a counter or put the noun first instead (병사 {troops}명, 금 {gold}, 병량 {food}, {days}일, {months}개월, 도시 {count}곳, 무장 {count}명).",
    "07_placeholders": "Keep every {placeholder} with its exact name and count; reorder freely for Korean word order (verb last). Drop the English article before factions: 'the {faction}' -> '{faction}'. Repeated placeholders (e.g. {unit} twice, {talent} twice) may be kept twice or the second replaced by a pronoun-free rephrase, but the placeholder set must stay identical to English. Use a normal space between a placeholder and a following noun ('{city} 공성전', '병력 {troops}').",
    "08_numbers_dates": "Dates: 'Year {year}, Month {month}, Day {day}' -> '{year}년 {month}월 {day}일'; 'Year {year}, Month {month} ({season})' -> '{year}년 {month}월 ({season})'; battle 'Day {day}' -> '{day}일째'; 'Day {day} of {days}' -> '{days}일 중 {day}일째'; duel 'Round {round}/{max}' -> '{round}/{max}합'; compact log dates '{year}.{month}' keep their dots. Percent stays '{chance}%'; '+{amount}' / '-{order}' keep their signs ('농업 +{amount}'); '{value} / {max}' unchanged; ages '{age}세'.",
    "09_names": "Officers, cities, factions, sects and techniques use exactly the Hangul forms in 'names' (Sino-Korean readings of the scenario nameZh), without spaces, middle dots, Hanja or romanization. 두음법칙 applies to surnames and to the first syllable of two-syllable given names (林 임, 雷 뇌, 盧/魯 노, 羅 나, 梁 양, 龍 용, 寧 영, 蘭 난, 陸 육, 凌 능: 도연산, 정난인, 사능소); single-syllable given names keep the original sound as in Korean 삼국지 (장료, 마량, 감녕 -> 연렬, 남궁렬, 곽린). Compound surnames: 혁련, 탁발, 모용, 독고, 상관, 구양, 사도, 동방, 남궁. 沈 surname = 심, 單 surname = 선, 釋 (monastic surname) = 석, 葉 = 엽. When English uses a given name alone ('Send Xiaoman') use the given-name syllables (소만). City 'Yanmen Pass' is 안문 as a name; in prose 'Yanmen Pass' (the pass itself) = 안문관, 'the Jade Gate' = 옥문관.",
    "10_titles": "Titles stand before the name in narration, record style: 승상 온정감, 대신 풍안, 제독 척봉, 황자 유승현, 당주 난월여, 방장 석혜명. In direct speech use the address terms instead (주공, 전하, 여왕 전하, 대칸, 형님). Officer epithets ('names' officer.*.epithet) are nicknames (별호) without articles; '{name}, {epithet}' -> '{epithet} {name}' (e.g. '북원의 매 모용소'). When prose refers to someone by epithet (the Grey Wolf, the Quick Knife), reuse the same wording (잿빛 늑대, 쾌도). Epithets are short 별호, not job descriptions, and must not open with a word that parses as a pronoun or verb (avoid '나는 ...', '우리 ...'): 날랜 깃털, 우리에 갇힌 범, 비단 공자, 무쇠 산, 대상 우두머리. Keep epithets to about 2-6 syllables: when a literal clause would run longer or read as a job description, use an established 별호 (Breaker of Armies = 만인적, the Far-Sighted = 천리안, Admiral of a Thousand Sails = 천범 제독, not '천 척 함대의 제독', whose counter 척 also echoes the surname in '{epithet} {name}').",
    "11_factions_glyphs": "Faction names exactly as in 'names': 철기군, 서랑기, 청정, 동해맹, 남련, 흑산채; short references: the League = 동해맹, the Brotherhood = 흑산채, the Court/Azure Court = 청정 (the imperial court in general = 조정). Banner glyphs (faction.<key>.glyph) stay single traditional Hanja: 燕 赫 劉 沈 蘇 墨. Unowned cities / faction.independent = 독립 세력.",
    "12_battle_locations": "battle.location.* values are inserted into {location} in other sentences, so they must be bare place noun phrases: 'Road to {city}' -> '{city} 가도', 'Siege of {city}' -> '{city} 성하', 'the battlefield' -> '전장'. Then 'The battle of {location}' -> '{location} 전투', 'at {location}' -> '{location}에서' (invariant particle).",
    "13_duel": "Duel / Single Combat = 일기토 everywhere in the game and in war prose, e.g. chieftains defeated in single combat (challenge = 일기토 신청, accept = 응전). Moves (초식): Strike 강타, Feint 허초, Guard 방어; rule hint: '강타는 허초를, 허초는 방어를, 방어는 강타를 이깁니다.' Technique = 무공 (sect-taught techniques; 'Techniques taught' = 전수 무공); HP = 체력; Round = 합. Exception: a bout between martial artists outside war (a duel inside a sect, sparring, a tournament match) is 비무, as in 정상 비무대회 and '비무 중에 상대를 죽인 탓에 운봉검파에서 파문당하자'.",
    "14_orders_vs_stats": "Keep each order paired with the attribute it raises: 농지 개간 -> 농업, 상업 진흥 -> 상업, 성벽 보수 -> 방어 (the walls themselves = 성벽), 순찰 -> 치안, 징병 -> 병력, 훈련 -> 훈련, 문파 수련 -> 무력 경험치, 친선 -> 신뢰, 포상 -> 충성. Order previews read '<attribute> +{amount}' (농업 +{amount}). Disambiguate: Order (an officer's assignment) = 명령 but Public Order = 치안; Command (stat) = 통솔 but the Command/Take Command battle buttons = 직접 지휘 and Commander = 대장; Recruit (persuade officers) = 등용 but Draft Troops = 징병.",
    "15_phases": "Planning phase = 계획 단계 (orders, saving, market and rewards happen here); the month playing out = 진행 단계. End Turn = 턴 종료 on both the strategy and the battle screen. Delegate = 위임 (city to a governor = 태수에게 위임; battle to the generals = 지휘 위임). A playthrough is 게임 (New Campaign = 새 게임, Load Campaign = 게임 불러오기); a military campaign in prose is 원정.",
    "16_wuxia_world": "Sect = 문파, sect master = 장문인, Moonwell's mistress = 당주 (월정당주), abbot = 방장, disciple = 제자. World: 구주 (Nine Provinces), 천하 (the realm), 천명 (the Mandate), 어린 황제 (boy emperor), 선제 (old emperor), 조정 (the court), 옛 황도 (old imperial capital), 대하 (Great River), 남강 (Southern River), 중원 (Central Plain). Martial vocabulary: 검 sword, 도 saber, 창 spear/lance, 장 palm, 권 fist, 침 needles, 무예 fighting art. Keep the setting Chinese: no Korea-specific folk terms (한가위, 추석, 설날); Mid-Autumn = 중추절, its full moon = 중추의 보름달.",
    "17_punctuation": "Korean uses ASCII punctuation: '.', ',', '!', '?', ':', '...', straight or curly quotes as in the English source; parentheses stay ASCII '( )'. Keep English separators such as ' - ' and ': ' in UI lines, '{column} ^ / v' sort markers, '1x/2x/4x' and the '-' empty-value dash unchanged.",
    "18_consistency": "Use exactly one rendering per English term across all four chunks (see 'terms'; parenthesised notes in the English keys only disambiguate senses). Never invent alternative names for people, places, factions, sects or techniques, and keep spacing exactly as listed (운봉검파, 석종사, 월정당, 흑산 산맥). Avoid homographs of established game terms: 장수 always means 將帥 (general), so a trader is 거간꾼/상인 (the Horse Trader = 말 거간꾼); 노병 is 弩兵 (노병대 crossbowmen), so veterans = 옛 병사/백발의 병사; 은자 is the hermit (隱者), so silver sent as a gift = 은전 (only the innkeeper's idiom '은자 한 닢' in ui.strategy.noRumor keeps 은자).",
    "19_region_labels": "Map region labels coincide with some city names by design (the pinyin city names literally mean those regions): label 동해 (Eastern Sea) = city 동해 (Donghai); 흑산 산맥 (Black Mountains) vs city 흑산 (Heishan) vs faction 흑산채; 옥문 (Yumen) vs 옥문관 (the Jade Gate in prose); 진천 (Qinchuan) vs 진령 산맥 (Qin Mountains). Sea League 동해맹 is the faction, never shortened to 동해."
  },
